### What are the two proxy types in Spring?

Spring creates an AOP proxy around your bean to implement `@Transactional`. There are two proxy mechanisms.

A JDK dynamic proxy is created by `java.lang.reflect.Proxy`. It works only when your bean implements at least one interface because the proxy itself implements those interfaces. It reads transaction metadata from the interface methods.

A CGLIB proxy is created by generating a subclass of your concrete class at runtime. It does not need an interface because it extends the class directly. It reads transaction metadata from the concrete class and its methods.

```java
package com.bank.service;

// Interface
public interface TransferService {
    void transfer(String fromId, String toId, java.math.BigDecimal amount);
}

// Implementation
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferServiceImpl implements TransferService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public TransferServiceImpl(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
    @Override
    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account sender = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
        Account receiver = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId);
        sender.debit(amount);
        receiver.credit(amount);
        accountRepo.save(sender);
        accountRepo.save(receiver);
    }
}
```

### Why does Spring Boot default to CGLIB?

Historically, Spring used JDK proxies when the bean had an interface and fell back to CGLIB only when there was no interface. Since Spring 5 and Spring Boot 2, the default changed to `proxyTargetClass = true`, meaning CGLIB is used even when interfaces are present.

The reason is consistency: with CGLIB as the default, beans behave the same regardless of whether they implement an interface. You inject the concrete type directly without worrying about type casting issues.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@RestController
public class TransferController {

    // With CGLIB, you can inject the concrete type directly
    private final TransferServiceImpl transferService;

    public TransferController(TransferServiceImpl transferService) {
        this.transferService = transferService;
    }

    @PostMapping("/transfer")
    public String transfer() {
        transferService.transfer("SAV2001", "SAV3002",
            new BigDecimal("50000"));
        return "Transfer complete";
    }
}
```

### How do JDK and CGLIB proxies compare?

| Aspect | JDK Dynamic Proxy | CGLIB Proxy |
|--------|-------------------|-------------|
| Mechanism | Implements interfaces | Subclasses concrete class |
| Interface required | Yes | No |
| Reads annotations from | Interface methods | Implementation class |
| final class | Works (proxy is separate) | Fails (cannot subclass final) |
| final method | Proxied (if on interface) | Not proxied |
| Private method | Not proxied | Not proxied |
| Spring Boot default | No | Yes (proxyTargetClass=true) |
| Injection type | Must inject interface type | Can inject concrete type |

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.EnableTransactionManagement;

// To force JDK proxies (not recommended for Spring Boot):
@Configuration
@EnableTransactionManagement(proxyTargetClass = false)
public class JdkProxyConfig {
    // Beans implementing interfaces will use JDK dynamic proxy
    // Beans without interfaces still use CGLIB
}
```

### Why should @Transactional go on the implementation?

A CGLIB proxy subclasses the implementation. It reliably sees `@Transactional` on the implementation class and its methods. It does not reliably pick up annotations that exist only on the interface, because CGLIB operates at the class level.

If you put `@Transactional` only on the interface and Spring uses CGLIB, the annotation can be silently ignored. No transaction starts, and your transfer method runs without atomicity. In a banking application, this means a failing transfer could leave money half-moved.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

// WRONG: annotation on the interface, CGLIB may ignore it
public interface TransferService {
    @Transactional
    void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount);
}

// Implementation has no annotation
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class TransferServiceImpl implements TransferService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public TransferServiceImpl(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Override
    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // No transaction: debit succeeds, credit fails,
        // Rajesh loses Rs.50,000 and Priya never receives it
        Account sender = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
        Account receiver = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId);
        sender.debit(amount);
        receiver.credit(amount);
    }
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: annotation on the implementation
public interface TransferService {
    void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount);
}

@Service
public class TransferServiceImpl implements TransferService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public TransferServiceImpl(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
    @Override
    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account sender = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
        Account receiver = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId);
        sender.debit(amount);
        receiver.credit(amount);
    }
}
```

### What about final classes and methods?

CGLIB creates a subclass, so the target class must not be `final`. A `final` class cannot be subclassed, and Spring will fail at startup or fall back silently. Similarly, a `final` method cannot be overridden by the CGLIB subclass, so its `@Transactional` is ignored.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

// WRONG: final class cannot be proxied by CGLIB
@Service
public final class FinalTransferService {

    @Transactional
    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // CGLIB cannot create a subclass of a final class
    }
}
```

```java
// WRONG: final method cannot be overridden by CGLIB
@Service
public class TransferService {

    @Transactional
    public final void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // @Transactional is silently ignored
    }
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: non-final class and non-final method
@Service
public class TransferService {

    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // CGLIB can proxy this
    }
}
```

### What about private methods?

Neither JDK nor CGLIB proxies can intercept private methods. A `@Transactional` annotation on a private method is silently ignored. The method runs without a transaction.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class FeeService {

    // WRONG: private method, annotation ignored
    @Transactional
    private void calculateFee(String accountId) {
        // runs without transaction
    }

    // CORRECT: public method, proxy intercepts
    @Transactional
    public void calculateFee(String accountId, java.math.BigDecimal rate) {
        // runs inside a transaction
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake 1: annotating only the interface

```java
// WRONG: @Transactional on interface, CGLIB ignores it
public interface LoanService {
    @Transactional
    void disburseLoan(String loanId);
}

@Service
public class LoanServiceImpl implements LoanService {
    @Override
    public void disburseLoan(String loanId) { /* no TX */ }
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: @Transactional on implementation
public interface LoanService {
    void disburseLoan(String loanId);
}

@Service
public class LoanServiceImpl implements LoanService {
    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
    @Override
    public void disburseLoan(String loanId) { /* TX active */ }
}
```

#### Mistake 2: making the service class final (Kotlin default)

In Kotlin, classes are `final` by default. You must use the `open` keyword or the `kotlin-allopen` compiler plugin to make CGLIB proxying work.

```kotlin
// WRONG in Kotlin: class is final by default
@Service
class TransferService {
    @Transactional
    fun transfer(fromId: String, toId: String, amount: BigDecimal) { }
}

// CORRECT in Kotlin: open class
@Service
open class TransferService {
    @Transactional
    open fun transfer(fromId: String, toId: String, amount: BigDecimal) { }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The Spring Framework reference documentation itself recommends annotating concrete classes rather than interfaces because the choice of proxy strategy affects whether interface annotations are honored.
- You can force JDK proxies with `@EnableTransactionManagement(proxyTargetClass = false)`, but even then annotating the implementation is the defensive choice.
- Spring's `AnnotationTransactionAttributeSource` does check interfaces as a fallback for JDK proxies, but this behavior is not guaranteed across proxy types.
- In Spring Boot 3.x, CGLIB proxy generation is further optimized. The class must still be non-final.
- For Kotlin projects, use the `kotlin-spring` compiler plugin (included in the Spring Boot Kotlin starter) which automatically opens classes annotated with `@Service`, `@Component`, and `@Transactional`.

```java
// Check at runtime which proxy type Spring created
import org.springframework.aop.support.AopUtils;

public class ProxyCheck {
    public static void checkProxy(Object bean) {
        System.out.println("Is CGLIB: " + AopUtils.isCglibProxy(bean));
        System.out.println("Is JDK: " + AopUtils.isJdkDynamicProxy(bean));
        System.out.println("Proxy class: " + bean.getClass().getName());
    }
}
```

